          "type": "turn",
          "question": "What is the difference between flu and a cold?",
          "answer": "A cold and the flu are both viral infections of the nose and throat, but they usually feel different.\n\n**A cold** (an upper respiratory infection, per the \"Upper respiratory infection (common cold)\" summary) usually starts gradually. Its main symptoms are a runny or blocked nose and a sore throat. High fever is uncommon in adults. Most healthy people recover on their own.\n\n**Influenza** (per the \"Influenza\" summary) usually starts suddenly, and many people can name the hour it began. Typical symptoms are fever, chills, muscle aches, headache, marked tiredness and a dry cough. It is generally more severe than a cold and can lead to hospitalization, especially in older adults, young children, pregnant people and people with long-term conditions.\n\nThe guidance notes that these differences are useful clues but not reliable enough to tell them apart with certainty in an individual. A clinician can help work out which one is more likely.\n\nThe sources also mention that yellow or green nasal discharge is a normal part of a viral cold and does not by itself mean a bacterial infection (per the antibiotic stewardship guidance). Whether any treatment is needed is a question for a clinician.\n\n**Seek emergency care right away** if someone has fever with a severe headache and stiff neck, a rash of spots that does not fade when pressed, confusion or difficulty waking, or, in a baby, a high-pitched cry, refusing feeds, or a bulging soft spot on the head (per the meningitis red-flag guidance).\n\nThe search did not return information on how flu and colds are treated or prevented, such as vaccination, so I can't speak to that here.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
